;; The threaded way (HES84 (a), doc/haskell/HASKELL-EVAL-SURVEY.md "As built: HES84 (a), the threaded way"): a program GHC
;; links -threaded (hsi run --threaded) runs with the threaded RTS's semantics where a program can observe them, staged:
;;   - capabilities: enabled_capabilities is +RTS -N's (default 1), setNumCapabilities changes it (clamped to the larger of -N
;;     and the host's processors, as Capability.c's max_n_capabilities), forkOn's capability is its number modulo it; the
;;     Haskell threads are still evaluated by one goroutine, the capability every thread runs on in turn (stage 1: no two
;;     Haskell threads in parallel, which no judged output depends on);
;;   - safe foreign calls release the capability (Schedule.c's suspendThread and resumeThread): a safe call to a host function
;;     that only blocks (sleep, usleep, nanosleep) runs on a goroutine of its own while the other threads run, its thread
;;     BlockedOnCCall until it returns; an asynchronous exception thrown to it waits for the return, as GHC's (a non-interruptible
;;     call). Unsafe calls, and safe calls into the program's own C (ci's interpreter is the machine's), keep the capability;
;;   - bound threads: the main thread, forkOS's (forkOS_createThread) and the in-calls' threads are bound
;;     (isCurrentThreadBound#); rtsSupportsBoundThreads answers 1 to GHC.Internal.Conc.Bound and the program's own modules, and
;;     0 to the I/O manager's modules (GHC.Internal.Conc.IO's threadDelay and threadWaitRead, IO.FD, System.Timeout, the
;;     signals): the I/O manager stays hsi's own, the non-threaded RTS's awaitEvent over delay#, waitRead# and waitWrite# (the
;;     threaded RTS's Haskell event manager over epoll is not ported: the same waits, by another mechanism);
;;   - sparks: a pool of at most 4096 (GHC's maxLocalSparks) unevaluated closures, numSparks# its size; an idle scheduler runs
;;     them one at a time, as an idle capability's spark thread;
;;   - hs_exit (and the end of main): the standard handles flushed, then exitScheduler's shutdown: the other threads deleted,
;;     those in foreign calls left to return into a stopped RTS (their goroutines' results dropped).


